w('search-response-3.json',"\"Polynomial Relations Between Matrices of Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03298)\\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.4 years ago; Title: Polynomial Relations Between Matrices of Graphs ... We derive a correspondence between the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix $A$ and the signless Laplacian matrix $Q$ of a graph $G$ when $G$ is $(d_1,d_2)$-biregular by using the relation $A^2=(Q-d_1I)(Q-d_2I)$.\\nTitle: Polynomial Relations Between Matrices of Graphs\\nAuthors: Sam Spiro\\nDate: Sun Jun 11 02:22:30 2017\\n\\nWe derive a correspondence between the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix $A$ and the signless Laplacian matrix $Q$ of a graph $G$ when $G$ is $(d_1,d_2)$-biregular by using the relation $A^2=(Q-d_1I)(Q-d_2I)$. This motivates asking when it is possible to have $X^r=f(Y)$ for $f$ a polynomial, $r>0$, and $X,\\\\ Y$ matrices associated to a graph $G$. It turns out that, essentially, this can only happen if $G$ is either regular or biregular.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nProblems that I would like Somebody to Solve (https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~sspiro/Research/Problems2Solve.pdf)\\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Polynomial Relations Between Matrices of Graphs ... matrix of G, D its diagonal matrix of degrees (i.e. ... If G is a graph and Ar = f(L) for some integer r ≥1 and polynomial ... In [10] I prove that this conjecture implies Conjecture 3.1, and computational data strongly\\nProblem 2.2. Improve upon either of these bounds for E[ex(H3\\nn,n−2/3, C3\\n4)].\\nMubayi and Yepremyan [7] conjecture that the lower bound from the deletion argument is\\nessentially best possible, and they provide some evidence that points in this direction. On the\\nother hand, Nie, Verstra¨ete, and myself [8] proved that the analogous problem for loose triangles\\nhas a stronger lower bound than what you get from a deletion argument, which suggests that\\nimprovements might be possible for all loose cycles. Overall it’s very unclear what the correct\\nanswer should be here.\\n3\\nSome Smaller Problems\\n3.1\\nPolynomial Relations Between Matrices of Graphs\\nThe general question is: given two matrices X, Y associated to a graph G, does there exist\\nan integer r and a polynomial f such that Xr = f(Y )? For example, let A be the adjacency\\nmatrix of G, D its diagonal matrix of degrees (i.e. Duu = du), and L = D −A its combinatorial\\nLaplacian. If G is d-regular, then we have A = dI −L, so we get a positive answer with r = 1\\nand f(x) = d −x. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n10.5. THE RIGHT EIGENVECTORS OF GRAPH MATRICES 425 (https://turan-edu.uz/media/books/2024/05/28/1660642748.pdf)\\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.5 years ago; Crawled: 6.5 years ago; Lemma 10.5.1 Let \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) be the degree matrix of adjacency matrix \\\\(A\\\\). ... Corollary 10.5.1 The first eigenvector of the symmetrically normalized adjacency matrix \\\\(S = \\\\Delta^{-1/2}A\\\\Delta^{-1/2}\\\\) is proportional to an \\\\(n\\\\)-dimensional vector containing the square-roots of the weighted vertex degrees.\\nThen, \\\\(\\\\bar{x}\\\\) is an eigenvector of \\\\(P\\\\) with eigenvalue \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\), if and only if \\\\(\\\\sqrt{\\\\Delta}\\\\bar{x}\\\\) is an eigenvector of \\\\(S\\\\) with eigenvalue \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\).\\n\\nProof: We show that both of the above statements are true if and only if \\\\(\\\\bar{x}\\\\) is a generalized eigenvector of \\\\(A\\\\) satisfying \\\\(A\\\\bar{x} = \\\\lambda \\\\Delta \\\\bar{x}\\\\).\\n\\nFirst, we note that \\\\(P\\\\bar{x} = \\\\lambda \\\\bar{x}\\\\) is true if and only if \\\\(\\\\Delta^{-1}A\\\\bar{x} = \\\\lambda \\\\bar{x}\\\\), which is the same as saying that \\\\(A\\\\bar{x} = \\\\lambda \\\\Delta \\\\bar{x}\\\\).\\n\\nSecond, we note that \\\\(S[\\\\sqrt{\\\\Delta}\\\\bar{x}] = \\\\lambda[\\\\sqrt{\\\\Delta}\\\\bar{x}]\\\\) is true if and only if \\\\(\\\\Delta^{-1/2}A\\\\bar{x} = \\\\lambda[\\\\sqrt{\\\\Delta}\\\\bar{x}]\\\\), which is the same as saying that \\\\(A\\\\bar{x} = \\\\lambda \\\\Delta \\\\bar{x}\\\\). This completes the proof.\\n\\nSince the first eigenvector of the Shi-Malik embedding is a column of 1s, it follows that the first eigenvector of the Ng-Jordan-Weiss embedding is proportional to \\\\(\\\\sqrt{\\\\Delta}[1,1,\\\\ldots,1]^T\\\\), which is the same as \\\\([\\\\sqrt{d_1}, \\\\sqrt{d_2}, \\\\ldots, \\\\sqrt{d_n}]^T\\\\).\\n\\nCorollary 10.5.1 The first eigenvector of the symmetrically normalized adjacency matrix \\\\(S = \\\\Delta^{-1/2}A\\\\Delta^{-1/2}\\\\) is proportional to an \\\\(n\\\\)-dimensional vector containing the square-roots of the weighted vertex degrees.\\n\\nAn important point is that the Ng-Jordan-Weiss embedding normalizes the rows before applying \\\\(k\\\\)-means, whereas the Shi-Malik approach does not normalize the rows before applying \\\\(k\\\\)-means. In other words, the former is row-normalized before applying \\\\(k\\\\)-means, whereas the latter is column-normalized before applying \\\\(k\\\\)-means. This implies the following unified extraction of both embeddings using the symmetric kernel matrix \\\\(S = \\\\Delta^{-1/2}A\\\\Delta^{-1/2}\\\\).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nRegular-or-biregular conjecture for powers of the adjacency matrix and normalized Laplacian | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/336330/regular-or-biregular-conjecture-for-powers-of-the-adjacency)\\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Let $G$G be a connected graph, let $A$A be its adjacency matrix, and let $\\\\mathcal{L}$L be its normalized Laplacian. ... The conjecture is motivated by the lack of a complete characterization of the relation $A^r=f(\\\\mathcal{L})$A r=f(L); the paper proves the conclusion when $r$r is odd, but the general case remains open. ... Sam Spiro, “Polynomial Relations Between Matrices of Graphs”, arXiv:1706.03298 (2017).\\n\\nRegular-or-biregular conjecture for powers of the adjacency matrix and normalized Laplacian\\n\\nAt least 8 years old · documented by 2017\\n\\nLet $G$G be a connected graph, let $A$A be its adjacency matrix, and let $\\\\mathcal{L}$L be its normalized Laplacian. A graph is regular if all its vertices have the same degree, and biregular if its vertices have two degree values such that every edge joins vertices of different degree values. Regular-or-biregular conjecture. If, for some polynomial $f$f and integer $r>0$r>0,\\n\\n$$A^r=f(\\\\mathcal{L}),$$A r=f(L),\\n\\nthen $G$G is regular or biregular. The conjecture is motivated by the lack of a complete characterization of the relation $A^r=f(\\\\mathcal{L})$A r=f(L); the paper proves the conclusion when $r$r is odd, but the general case remains open.\\n\\n## Primary source\\n\\nSam Spiro, “Polynomial Relations Between Matrices of Graphs”, arXiv:1706.03298 (2017).\\n\\ngraph-matricesspectral-graph-theory\\n\\n\\n3 weeks ago\\n\\n## Progress summary\\n\\nRefresh Never refreshed\\n\\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\\n\\n## Solutions 0\\n\\n[Button: Post a solution]\\n\\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\\n\\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nLNCS 4123 - Remarks on an Edge Isoperimetric Problem (https://www.math.uni-luebeck.de/mitarbeiter/bey/pub/remarks_on_an_edge_isoperimetric_problem__06_BEY.pdf)\\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.9 years ago; Let G = (V, E) be a connected semiregular graph with bipartition\\nV = V0 ∪V1 and degrees d0, d1. ... adjacency matrix of G. ... tive and negative) square roots of the nonzero eigenvalues of W ⊤W, with equal\\nRemarks on an Edge Isoperimetric Problem\\n973\\nTheorem 2. Le