\mu \\\\mathbf{w}$$\\n\\nthen $\\\\lambda = 1-\\\\mu$ and $\\\\mathbf{z} = \\\\mathbf{w} \\\\mathbb{G}^{-1/2}$. The square roots come in only at the end as easy matrix multiplications, while $\\\\mathbb{GA}$ is a rational matrix. Hopefully, calculations involving it will be faster.\\n\\n### Solution\\n\\nHere is an implementation:\\n    \\n    `f[g_, a_] := Module[{e, v},\\n       {e, v} = Eigensystem[DiagonalMatrix[1/g] . a];\\n       {1 - e, v.DiagonalMatrix[Sqrt[g]]}\\n       ];\\n    `\\n\\nLet's carry it out on the example data:\\n    \\n    `g = ExampleData[{\\\"NetworkGraph\\\", \\\"ZacharysKarateClub\\\"}];\\n    {l, z} = f[VertexDegree[g], AdjacencyMatrix[g]]; // AbsoluteTiming\\n    `\\n\\n> {31.8538219, Null}\\n\\n* * *\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nOn the Extended Adjacency Eigenvalues of Graphs and Applications (https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/13/22/3620)\\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Since the graph ${\\\\mathcal{G}}_{i}$ is a ${r}_{i}$-regular graph of order ${n}_{i}$, for all $i=1,2,\\\\dots ,n$, the all one-vector ${e}_{{n}_{i}}={(1,1,\\\\dots ,1)}^{T}$ is the eigenvector of the adjacency matrix $A\\\\left({\\\\mathcal{G}}_{i}\\\\right)$ corresponding to the eigenvalue ${r}_{i}$, and all other eigenvectors are orthogonal to ${e}_{{n}_{i}}$ (as the matrix $A\\\\left({\\\\mathcal{G}}_{i}\\\\right)$ being symmetric is orthogonally diagonalizable).\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nHandbook of Network Analysis (https://abcdocz.com/doc/1391383/handbook-of-network-analysis)\\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.5 years ago; Crawled: last month;     Normalized Adjacency Matrix (N) ...     When the graph is connected, the eigenvector corresponding to eigenvalue\\n    one has entries proportional to the square root of node degrees, i.e., ...     the graph is undirected, except when the gra